. Both ways kick off through the same engine, so there is no penalty for skipping, but his changes can move the result the same way yours can. A Champions League final that is level after 90, or a second leg that leaves the tie level on aggregate, plays thirty minutes of extra time before penalties, with the clock running on to 120 and your changes still open. In the eras that had the away goals rule they come first: a tie level on aggregate but not on away goals is over at 90, and an away goal in extra time still counts, so the home side then needs two. Your domestic cup still goes straight to penalties, because the real cups do not all play extra time. The board is real time too: the clock runs on to 45+2' or 90+5' and goals, cards and injuries can land in it, and on the second leg of a two legged tie the first leg and the running aggregate sit under the score so you always know what the tie says, not just the night. The report gives you the scoreline with the stoppage time both halves ran to, scorers, cards and subs on both sides, your injuries, possession as the two shares of the ball, shots, expected goals, a momentum graph drawn from who had the chances in each ten minutes, and every player's rating on both sides. The Match Centre shows both clubs' form, your past meetings and the engine's own win odds before you commit. Every match screen names the home side first, the way a scoreboard does: the next match card, the Match Centre, the live score, half time and the report all read home against away, with each club's stats, scorers and timeline under its own name, so away from home your club sits on the right, picked out in green.</p>
